  "enhanced_information": "The patient has low systolic blood pressure (70 mmHg) and bradycardia (heart rate of 45 beats/minute) following a traffic accident, which suggests a state of shock. The hot skin of the extremities indicates possible vasodilation. The most likely cause of shock in this scenario is neurogenic shock, which can occur due to spinal cord injury leading to loss of sympathetic tone and resultant vasodilation. Hypovolemic shock is also a consideration due to potential blood loss from the accident, but the presence of hot skin and bradycardia aligns more closely with neurogenic shock. Intrinsic cardiogenic shock is less likely without evidence of cardiac injury, and septic shock is unlikely given the acute nature of the incident and lack of infection signs. Obstructive cardiogenic shock is also unlikely without specific evidence of obstruction. Therefore, neurogenic shock is the most supported diagnosis based on the clinical presentation."
